      "functional_impact_measured": "CD81::GFP::Spike fusion constructs supported SARS-CoV-2 Spike-mediated membrane fusion in cell-based assays, confirming the insertion at 179\u2013180 is tolerated for membrane trafficking and surface display. CD81::GFP alone (pDD3513) was also produced and characterized.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "L1 explicitly names residues 179\u2013180 of CD81 (UniProt P60033) as the insertion junction for both GFP and Spike domain fusions in full-length, membrane-anchored CD81. Python verification confirms residue 179 = S and residue 180 = N, both within the large extracellular loop ECL2 (topology landmarks 114\u2013201), 66 aa from the TM3 boundary and 22 aa from the TM4 boundary. The constructs were functionally characterized in viral entry assays, establishing surface display and functional tolerance. Full-length CD81 constructs (CD81::GFP, pDD3513; CD81::GFP::D614G S WA1, pDD3515; and variants) were engineered by inserting GFP and/or the SARS-CoV-2 Spike ectodomain between CD81 residues 179 and 180 (UniProt P60033 numbering). Constructs were expressed in mammalian cells and used in SARS-CoV-2 entry/fusion assays.",
